</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Emilio Barkett
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Columbia University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">eab2291@columbia.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">As large language models (LLMs) become increasingly embedded in products used by millions, their outputs may influence individual beliefs and, cumulatively, shape public opinion. If the behavior of LLMs can be intentionally steered toward specific ideological positions, such as political or religious views, then those who control these systems could gain disproportionate influence over public discourse. Although it remains an open question whether LLMs can reliably be guided toward coherent ideological stances and whether such steering can be effectively prevented, a crucial first step is to develop methods for detecting when such steering attempts occur. In this work, we adapt a previously proposed statistical method to the new context of ideological bias auditing. Our approach carries over the model-agnostic design of the original framework, which does not require access to the internals of the language model. Instead, it identifies potential ideological steering by analyzing distributional shifts in model outputs across prompts that are thematically related to a chosen topic. This design makes the method particularly suitable for auditing proprietary black-box systems. We validate our approach through a series of experiments, demonstrating its practical applicability and its potential to support independent post hoc audits of LLM behavior.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs have become core components of many digital services, powering chatbots, coding assistants, search engines, and more <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guu et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. In particular, chatbots are increasingly more capable of helping users research and form opinions by generating summaries, explanations, and arguments. Because these models may influence users’ beliefs, it is essential that their outputs remain factual, balanced, and free from systematic bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bolukbasi et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caliskan et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bender et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Motoki et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From a societal perspective, the widespread adoption of LLMs raises the crucial question of whether individuals or corporations that control these systems could intentionally steer them toward specific ideological positions in an attempt to influence their users’ opinions. Even though it is still an open question whether LLMs can reliably be guided toward a consistent ideological stance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santurkar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ceron et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, it is conceivable that providers may attempt to do so, especially through lightweight interventions such as modifying the system prompt. For instance, a provider might try to bias a model toward conservative Christian values, potentially influencing its responses on issues such as abortion.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          While current techniques may not ensure that a model consistently reflects a given ideological identity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santurkar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, this does not necessarily prevent providers from attempting such steering. One practical and low-effort mechanism for influence is the system prompt. For example, see the recent controversy regarding xAI’s Grok model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goggin (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vanian (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steedman (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">O’Brien (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morrow (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robertson (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conger (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kerr (2025)</a></cite>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Whether such interventions should be legally restricted is ultimately a regulatory question. However, the mere possibility that a few powerful actors could significantly influence public opinion poses a societal risk. As a first step toward addressing this issue, we propose a method for monitoring whether such steering occurs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we repurpose the training-free, model-agnostic framework introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite>, originally designed for detecting unauthorized use of proprietary system prompts. We apply this framework to the task of ideological bias auditing by monitoring whether a model’s responses to prompts on sensitive topics remain consistent over time. More concretely, we assume a fixed LLM and aim to detect steering attempts that result in a change in model outputs. The approach requires no access to model internals and is suitable for black-box settings, making it well-suited for independent post hoc audits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each sensitive topic of interest, we generate a set of task prompts and track model outputs over time. If responses exhibit significant distributional shifts, the system flags the change for further inspection. The framework is summarized in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12652v1/LaTeX/figures/AAAI.jpg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/248;" width="238" height="248" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Our general framework works as follows: We assume a base LLM deployed via a chat interface. To monitor whether this model’s outputs remain consistent over time, we generate topic-specific prompts and sample responses periodically. If the provider makes changes to the model (e.g., via a system prompt), trying to steer its behavior, our framework identifies distributional shifts in the outputs and alerts the user. This allows independent audits of potential ideological manipulation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">One might ask whether systematic monitoring is truly necessary, given that users could probably recognize drastic changes in model behavior on their own. However, there are several challenges that make this complicated in practice. Even subtle shifts in framing or emphasis, potentially unrecognizable to humans, can meaningfully affect human judgments and opinions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky and Kahneman (1981)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Entman (2006)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chong and Druckman (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beratšová et al. (2016)</a></cite>. Furthermore, without a principled approach, it is difficult to determine whether variations in model responses reflect inherent stochasticity or indicate a more fundamental change in behavior. Our framework addresses this problem by applying the statistical hypothesis test proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite> with a user-defined significance threshold <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling more reliable detection of distributional shifts. Manual inspection also does not scale well when attempting to monitor model outputs across a wide range of topics, further underscoring the need for automated auditing techniques.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the issues discussed above, we provide an example in the Appendix, where two responses are generated from the same model and for the same task prompt but with different system prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we make two primary contributions. First, we adapt a well-established, training-free statistical method to the novel setting of detecting ideological steering in LLMs. Second, we demonstrate the practical utility of our approach through a series of experiments. While the results are encouraging, we outline important limitations and directions for future work in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Limitations and Future Work ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While still an area of active research, biases in artificial intelligence systems have been rigorously examined <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bolukbasi et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gordon et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raghavan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Norori et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Numerous studies have identified systematic biases in LLMs, producing a wide range of interesting findings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bang et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bender et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nadeem et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hosseini et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schramowski et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. One notable result is that these models tend to express left-leaning or liberal views on social issues, while often exhibiting libertarian attitudes on economic topics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Motoki et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rozado, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Exler et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santurkar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rettenberger et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. To uncover these patterns, researchers frequently employ sophisticated prompting strategies, often using datasets designed to capture human political and cultural opinions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azzopardi and Moshfeghi, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caliskan et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although these methods provide valuable insights into model biases and facilitate cross-model comparisons, they were not developed to monitor how a single model’s outputs change over time and to detect sudden shifts in behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In theory, one could repeatedly apply these established bias-auditing techniques to track distributional changes in a deployed model. However, these methods were not designed or evaluated for ongoing monitoring, and their sensitivity to subtle shifts is unclear. Moreover, such approaches are likely to be computationally more expensive than the lightweight monitoring framework proposed in this work, which is specifically intended for efficient detection of distributional changes over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A central challenge in evaluating any monitoring framework is establishing a realistic way to simulate interventions that might steer model behavior in deployment. For this purpose, we rely on modifying the system prompt, since this method is both straightforward and effective in practice. The system prompt has a well-documented influence on the outputs of LLMs. Even small adjustments to the prompt can meaningfully alter both the tone and the substance of responses, affecting performance across a wide range of tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In addition to conveying high-level instructions, system prompts frequently include elements of in-context learning, such as zero-shot or few-shot examples, which allow models to adapt to new tasks at inference time without retraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Because these design choices can substantially impact model behavior, prompt engineering has become an important and often complex part of model deployment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulhoff et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. While some prompt collections are publicly available, system prompts used in commercial models are often proprietary and not disclosed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI Team, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic Team, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The opacity of system prompts has motivated the development of techniques for reconstructing or inferring hidden prompt content by eliciting information directly from the model’s outputs. Such methods are often referred to as prompt extraction attacks and raise concerns regarding prompt privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hui et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt membership inference addresses the question of whether two systems use the same underlying system prompt. This is particularly important in precisely these scenarios where a proprietary prompt may have been leaked or replicated without authorization. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite>, whose work lays the foundation for ours, approach this problem by proposing a statistical test to determine whether two sets of outputs are likely to have been generated under the same prompt. The task is formalized as a hypothesis test, where the null hypothesis assumes no difference between the two output distributions. Their method is model-agnostic and operates in a black-box setting, requiring access only to the generated responses, not to the model’s internal parameters or the prompts themselves. The present work builds directly on this methodology by applying it to a different problem setting. Rather than addressing prompt membership inference, we adapt the statistical test proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite> to monitor for changes in ideological alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section summarizes the statistical methodology introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite>, explains its adaptation to our context, and outlines our procedure for generating task-relevant prompts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Prompt Detective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite></figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
