<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Prompt Detective</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prompt Detective</span> is a training-free statistical method for determining whether the system prompt used by an LLM-based service matches a known reference prompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The approach compares distributions of model outputs generated under two different system prompts and applies a statistical hypothesis test to identify significant differences, thereby indicating whether the underlying prompts are distinct.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a language model <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, two system prompts <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a collection of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> task prompts <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{q_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{q_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the method proceeds as follows. For each task prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model is queried <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times under both <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding two sets of responses. These responses are then embedded using a sentence embedding model, such as BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers and Gurevych, 2019</a>)</cite>, to obtain high-dimensional semantic vectors. The mean embedding for each group is computed, and the cosine similarity between the two mean embeddings, denoted <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathrm{obs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>obs</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathrm{obs}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is used as the test statistic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To assess statistical significance, a permutation test <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Good, 2013</a>)</cite> is conducted. For each of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{perm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>perm</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{perm}}</annotation></semantics></math> permutations, the embeddings for each task prompt are pooled and randomly split into two new groups of size <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, from which new mean embeddings and cosine similarities are calculated. This process generates a null distribution of cosine similarities expected by chance. The <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-value is estimated as the proportion of permutations where the permuted similarity is less than or equal to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathrm{obs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>obs</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathrm{obs}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If this <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-value falls below a predefined significance level <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the test concludes that the two system prompts are distinct. The formal procedure is detailed in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This model-agnostic framework enables reliable detection of distributional changes using only generated model outputs. We adapt this method to monitor for distributional shifts in outputs over time, allowing us to detect potential steering or interventions in deployed LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Finding Task-Relevant Prompts</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To identify task-specific prompts with minimal manual effort, we leveraged GPT-4o via the OpenAI web interface<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            https://chatgpt.com/</span></span></span> to generate prompts relevant to each experiment. Our goal was to obtain general, open-ended questions. The prompt instructions for each experimental setting are summarized in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Finding Task-Relevant Prompts ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We did not experiment with alternative prompt sets systematically, yet consistently observed strong results across all our experiments. This suggests that the framework proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite> is robust and that suitable prompts can be identified with minimal prompt engineering effort. The questions generated for each experiment can be found in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S3.F2.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:338.1pt;">
          
<div id="S3.F2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F2.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="161.93" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 467.83 161.93" width="467.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,161.93) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000066;" fill="#000066" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 156.03 C 0 159.29 2.64 161.93 5.91 161.93 L 461.92 161.93 C 465.18 161.93 467.83 159.29 467.83 156.03 L 467.83 5.91 C 467.83 2.64 465.18 0 461.92 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F5F5FF;" fill="#F5F5FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 139.05 L 465.86 139.05 L 465.86 5.91 C 465.86 3.73 464.1 1.97 461.92 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 147.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.17em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="424.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.17em;">
<span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Instructions Used to Generate Task-Relevant Prompts</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.17em;--ltx-fo-height:8.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="113.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 111.04)" width="424.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.17em;">
<span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Religion Experiment:<span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <em id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">You may ask five questions to learn about a person’s religious views. The goal is not just to identify their specific beliefs or affiliations, but to gain a broader understanding of their general outlook on religion, such as how traditional or progressive their views might be. The questions should be phrased in a way that invites reflection or reaction, rather than directly asking for personal opinions.</em></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Conspiracy Experiment:<span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <em id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">You may ask five questions to understand a person’s political views. The goal is not just to determine their party affiliation, but to gain a broader sense of their overall political outlook—for example, how progressive or conservative they tend to be. The questions should be phrased in a general way to encourage thoughtful responses and reveal underlying attitudes, rather than directly asking for personal opinions.</em></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Grok Experiment:<span id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <em id="S3.F2.p1.pic1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">You may ask five questions to infer a person’s ideological views, including their political, social, and religious perspectives. The goal is to form a well-rounded picture of their worldview. Rather than asking for direct personal opinions, the questions should be phrased more generally to observe how the person responds or reacts.</em></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
        
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Prompt instructions used to generate task-relevant questions in each experiment. Example prompts and full question sets for each experiment are provided in the Appendix.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
