<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>
Detection results for all experiments. Each section summarizes performance metrics across all prompt pairs tested for the respective experiment and model. TP = true positives, FP = false positives, TN = true negatives, FN = false negatives. Avg <math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values refer to the p-value distributions in TP and TN cases, respectively.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the effectiveness of our framework, we simulate a real-world scenario in which a newly released LLM is accessed through a public chat interface. The goal is to monitor whether the model’s responses to prompts on sensitive topics remain stable over time. We focus on ideologically motivated changes to the system prompt, a simple yet realistic form of intervention that does not require costly retraining. This choice keeps resource requirements low, making it feasible given our constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each of our experiments begins by selecting a topic of interest and constructing corresponding task prompts, as described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Finding Task-Relevant Prompts ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. We then query the model to obtain an initial batch of responses, which serves as the null distribution and captures the model’s baseline behavior. While a real deployment would involve querying the model periodically over time, we simulate a distributional shift immediately by collecting a second batch of responses from the same model using a modified system prompt. To assess false negative rates, we include a control group where 25% of the prompt pairs are not subjected to any intervention and use the same system prompt in both batches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the two sets of responses, adapting the statistical test introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite> as described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Prompt Detective ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Although we simulate only a single step for potential interventions, practical deployments would require ongoing monitoring sampling multiple steps. In such settings, multiple hypothesis testing becomes an issue. Challenges associated with multiple hypothesis testing have been studied extensively in the statistical literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thomas (1993)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hochberg and Tamhane (1987)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout all experiments, we use a significance threshold of <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. The data, code, and all experimental results presented in this paper will be made publicly available on GitHub following the review process.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Religiously Motivated Manipulations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our first experiment, we simulate ideologically motivated interventions by modifying system prompts to introduce religious bias. We construct a synthetic dataset of system prompts pairs, where each pair contains a neutral prompt and a corresponding biased variant. These prompts span a wide range of religious ideologies. Details of the prompt generation process are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All prompts in this experiment are deliberately kept short and simple and do not reflect the complexity of real-world deployments. Furthermore, the biased prompts do instruct the model to obscure its religious perspective. An example prompt pair is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Finding Task-Relevant Prompts ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Results for this toy experiment are summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Finding Task-Relevant Prompts ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Across a range of models, our approach reliably detects distributional shifts resulting from religiously biased system prompts. This demonstrates that the statistical approach from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite> can be effectively applied to uncover ideologically motivated manipulations in this setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Subtle Political Manipulations via Conspiracy Framing</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we extend our evaluation to a politically sensitive domain. Specifically, we examine whether our approach can detect subtle ideological steering when a model is biased toward a particular conspiracy theory. As in the previous experiment, the same set of general-purpose political questions is used throughout and is agnostic to any specific conspiracy theory. Details on the task prompt construction are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each system prompt used in this experiment is designed to introduce a specific bias. Each biased prompt subtly frames the model as a believer in a particular conspiracy theory. In contrast to the previous experiment, the model is instructed not to mention its beliefs explicitly. Instead, the prompt guides the model to allow its worldview to influence its responses indirectly. An example pair of prompts is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Finding Task-Relevant Prompts ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, details are available in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The motivation for this setup is that conspiracy beliefs may affect the model’s responses only indirectly, except when directly asked about the relevant theory. This makes the detection task potentially more challenging, as we do not query the model about the conspiracy itself but instead about general political issues. Nonetheless, as shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Finding Task-Relevant Prompts ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the proposed approach reliably detects these potentially more subtle distributional shifts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Real-World System Prompt: Grok 4</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The preceding experiments relied on short, synthetic system prompts. Their design enabled us to generate a reasonable number of prompt pairs with minimal manual effort. However, it remains an open question whether the observed effects generalize to the longer and more nuanced prompts used in real-world deployments. To investigate this, we conduct an additional experiment using the system prompt from Grok 4, which is publicly available on xAI’s official GitHub repository <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI Team (2025)</a></cite>. Compared to synthetic prompts, the Grok 4 system prompt exhibits significantly greater length and complexity, making it more challenging to modify in a controlled manner at scale. To introduce ideological bias while preserving the structure and tone of the original, we manually construct a single intervention, the original Grok 4 prompt, and a modified variant biased toward a conservative Christian worldview. Both prompts are provided in the Appendix.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            There has been a known instance of a clearly biased system prompt in practice, during an incident with xAI’s Grok 3  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goggin (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vanian (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steedman (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">O’Brien (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morrow (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robertson (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conger (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kerr (2025)</a></cite>. However, the modified prompt responsible was never made public, preventing its use in our analysis.</span></span></span> The results in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Finding Task-Relevant Prompts ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Don’t Change My View: Ideological Bias Auditing in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> suggest that the proposed approach generalizes to more involved, production-grade system prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Limitations and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We believe that there is an essential societal interest for the research community to establish rigorous auditing frameworks capable of detecting ideological drift in LLMs. Such frameworks should provide methods that are robust to superficial or spurious variations in model outputs while remaining sensitive to subtle and meaningful changes in stance or framing. They should enable the detection of shifts both within a single model over time and allow for cross-model comparisons. They have to function in black-box settings and generalize across a wide range of potential steering mechanisms. We see this work as a first step toward building such an auditing framework, but several limitations remain to be addressed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, the detection method we adopt is highly sensitive to changes in output distributions and may flag differences that are not semantically meaningful. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite> show that even typographical errors in the system prompt can produce detectable shifts. However, such changes appear unlikely to affect the stance or articulation of a model on important issues. An effective auditing tool must distinguish between superficial variations and shifts that reflect changes in underlying values or ideological positions. One possible mitigation strategy is to analyze the model’s responses using methods that infer its alignment with specific political parties or value systems. For example, some works investigate the political orientations of various LLMs by comparing their responses to those of political parties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Motoki et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rozado, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Exler et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santurkar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rettenberger et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. A similar strategy could in principle be used to monitor how a model’s inferred positions shift over time. However, existing evaluations seem too costly to be run frequently, and may not be sensitive enough to detect subtle framing changes or shifts in emphasis that could still influence user interpretation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, our analysis focuses exclusively on a single class of interventions, namely changes to the system prompt. This setting offers a controlled and tractable test case, but it, of course, does not reflect the full range of ways in which models may be steered. In practice, model behavior can be influenced through fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agiza et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, modifications to the training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hall et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, retrieval-augmented generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guu et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, among other approaches. These mechanisms might be more difficult to isolate and evaluate, especially in black-box settings, and a comprehensive auditing framework should be designed to detect behavior changes resulting from a broad spectrum of intervention types.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Third, the empirical evaluation is limited in scope. We apply the method to a small number of prompts and topics, primarily to demonstrate proof of concept. Real-world auditing requires broader topical coverage, longer and more naturalistic model outputs, and sensitivity to complex forms of ideological framing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To advance this line of research, two directions are particularly important. First, new methods are needed for comparing model outputs that are robust to spurious variations yet sensitive to subtle, meaningful shifts, both within a single model over time and across different models. Second, the development of standardized benchmarks and shared datasets is crucial for enabling more efficient, transparent, and comparable evaluations of LLM auditing pipelines. We hope that this initial study helps motivate further work in this area and attracts both community attention and resources to support the development of rigorous auditing tools for ideological shifts in language models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work demonstrates that the training-free statistical framework developed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin et al. (2025)</a></cite> for prompt membership inference can be repurposed to detect some form of ideological steering in LLMs. We evaluate this approach in the specific context of system prompt changes and find that resulting shifts in model outputs can be identified reliably. While the method requires constructing topic-specific task prompts, doing so involves relatively little manual effort. Combined with the fact that it only necessitates access to model outputs, this approach is well-suited for black-box settings and enables scalable, automated, third-party auditing of deployed models. Although rigorous evaluation remains challenging due to the absence of well-established benchmarks for model manipulation, our experimental results indicate the potential for this method to detect basic attempts at ideological steering, if deployed at scale with appropriate resources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We view this contribution as an initial step toward a broader framework for monitoring value drift in LLMs. The long-term objective is to develop transparent, automated tools capable of detecting when models change their responses to sensitive topics. These tools should not only be able to detect behavioral changes within a single model over time, but also be able to compare across various models released by the same or different providers. Importantly, they must operate in black-box settings without requiring access to model internals or training data, enabling independent entities to perform extensible monitoring. Finally, such a framework must be robust enough to distinguish meaningful ideological shifts from spurious or irrelevant variations in model outputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
